  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Pressed Petal Card",
    "overview": "ORAIA looks like the card that comes with a bouquet: cream stock, deep rose ink, a soft hand-drawn serif for the names, and plenty of room around each line. The flowers in the photos supply the color; the interface stays in one rose family so the bouquets stay the brightest thing on the screen. Petals and small sparkles drift around the open spaces of the shop pages like loose petals fallen on a card, and never where someone has to read or tap.\n\nThe shop is soft and roomy. Steps come one at a time in big pill buttons and plain sentences, because the cadet ordering has likely never used a shop like this. Headings use Fraunces with its soft, slightly wonky cut, and everything people must read and act on (prices, forms, order details) uses the phone's own sans-serif, so it stays fast and familiar on a weak signal.\n\nThe admin is its own, quieter world. It keeps the cream page and rose ink so it still feels like ORAIA, but drops all ornament, sets work on white panels, uses Fraunces only for page titles and big numbers, and gives the company colors the job of telling orders apart. It is a tool for a rush on iPads and phones, not a showcase.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Cream page, never pure white; white is for cards, panels and fields.",
      "One ink: deep rose (petal-900), lightened with opacity for quieter text.",
      "One action color: rose (petal-700) on fully rounded pills.",
      "Fraunces with the soft, wonky cut for names and headings; the system sans-serif for everything else.",
      "Soft rose-tinted shadows under bouquet photos only.",
      "Decoration (petals, sparkles, a soft pink glow) on shop pages only, away from content."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Ink Rule",
        "body": "All text is Deep Rose Ink at some opacity. No black, no zinc gray: the neutral grays left in the base theme belong to the stock components, not to ORAIA.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Photos Bring the Color Rule",
        "body": "The interface stays rose-on-cream so the bouquets are the most colorful thing on any screen. New colors enter only as status or company meaning.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Serif Names, Sans Does Rule",
        "body": "Fraunces names things (bouquets, pages, sections, the brand). Anything a person reads to decide or acts on (prices, buttons, fields, order details, instructions) is the system sans.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Soft Cut Rule",
        "body": "Fraunces always carries the soft, wonky settings (`.type-soft`). A plain Fraunces heading is a mistake. Printed cards are the exception, where the plainer cut prints cleaner.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flowers Float Rule",
        "body": "Only bouquet photos and things floating over the page (dialogs, sheets, the drawer) cast shadows. Cards, panels and buttons stay flat and use borders.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** put pages on Card Cream (`#fbf7f0`); keep white for cards, panels and fields.",
      "**Do** write all text in Deep Rose Ink, using opacity for quieter levels.",
      "**Do** make every button, chip and nav link a full pill, with Garden Rose for the one main action.",
      "**Do** set names and headings in Fraunces with the soft cut, and everything people act on in the system sans.",
      "**Do** keep the admin quiet: no petals or sparkles, white panels, Fraunces only for page titles and big numbers.",
      "**Do** show company colors with the company name, as an outlined dot.",
      "**Do** keep touch targets 44px tall on phones and fields at 1rem so iOS doesn't zoom."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** use a pure white page background.",
      "**Don't** use military styling: no camouflage, stencil lettering, insignia, rank chevrons or badges, even though the customers are cadets.",
      "**Don't** let petals, sparkles or the pink glow sit on top of prices, buttons, fields or order details; ornament stays in empty space and is hidden from screen readers.",
      "**Don't** use the stock component grays or black for text; that is the base theme showing through.",
      "**Don't** add shadows to flat cards, panels or buttons.",
      "**Don't** use company colors as decoration or as brand accents."
    ]
  }
}